course: dictionary
lesson: 3834
title: Formal words: forthwith
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Forthwith is a formal word, so you will meet it in notices, rules, official letters and old stories. It means immediately, at once, without delay.
explain: «You must leave forthwith.» The sentence is a rule telling you to go. So forthwith means right now, not later. To check, swap in right now and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: People rarely say forthwith in everyday talk. Say right now or at once instead. Phrases such as with effect forthwith mean the change starts straight away.
fact: Forthwith means immediately, at once, without delay.
fact: Forthwith is an adverb, and it is formal. You will find it in official notices, laws and old stories.
fact: «You must leave forthwith.» means you must leave right now.
fact: In everyday talk, use right now or at once instead of forthwith.
fact: With effect forthwith means the new rule starts straight away.
exercise: «You must leave forthwith.» What does forthwith mean? | Whenever you feel like it | Quietly, on tiptoe | *Right now, without any delay | Only after your tea
exercise: «Report any damage forthwith.» Which everyday words could replace forthwith here? | sometime later | never | *at once | in the garden
exercise: Which everyday phrase means the same as forthwith? | next week | maybe | *right now | in a while
test: «The new rule takes effect forthwith.» Forthwith here means the rule starts | only on Sundays | at the end of the year | *straight away
test: Why would an official notice use forthwith? | Because it sounds like a bird | *Because it is a formal word used in official writing | Because it is the name of a town | Because it means later on
exam: «Pupils must hand in their forms forthwith.» Which word gives the best meaning of forthwith? | Eventually | Occasionally | *Immediately | Reluctantly
